  "denominator_notes": {
    "capture": "Counts are per inventory. Qin and UT 250 MS/s share part of a capture. The MCS classes partition 25 observed labels. Application messages count verified semantics.",
    "full": "Fixed E229 denominators for Qin MCS 66/70/85/88 after measured frame-end closure. E240 has two consecutive runs contributing 7 and 76 distinct new words. First allocation closures from the experiment reports.",
    "partial": "MCS denominators from E230 inventories. E247 additions exclude existing positive controls. Allocation closure concerns the systematic bytes.",
    "pipeline": "Three decoder branches use only the permanent mask, or that mask plus either of two disjoint whole-symbol sets. Gain-only and gain-plus-variance are separate estimation arms. Low-address parity contributes no decoder prior.",
    "mcs57_receiver": "PN15 comparisons use separately selected common received bits per experiment. CRC24 totals count unique coordinates, with four E178 directories and a two-coordinate overlap with the 35 directly read in E234. The E245 report deduplicates eight additional coordinates.",
    "mcs57_repeat": "Compared bits overlap on whole-symbol holdout per target. Native support and opposition count prior six-view erasures where two target low-margin branches agree. Donor and target share an archive group.",
    "aligned": "One high control per MCS and fold. High rows count whole-group holdout equations. Low rank and contradictions are from calibration. The 98 low-address models count all tested alignments and windows.",
    "headers": "The 51-event denominator merges 24 and 28 synchronized events with one overlap. E218 weak events have information and body-boundary support with unresolved CRC. LEO paired 2.5 MS/s results project 1,009 Qin H2 references conditionally. Ranks measure descriptor constraints.",
    "vhf": "Residual is observed minus predicted differential frequency. Split-preamble limits span two disjoint preamble halves under two downconversions. All estimates derive from the same pair of recorded packets. The span is descriptive."
  },
